<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A deliberately simpler classification and impairment model</div><div class="principle">ASPE Section 3856 governs the RECOGNITION, MEASUREMENT, and PRESENTATION of FINANCIAL instruments — FINANCIAL assets, FINANCIAL liabilities, and EQUITY instruments. GENUINELY distinctively, ASPE applies a MUCH simpler, TWO-category CLASSIFICATION model (Section 3) and an OLDER, INCURRED-loss impairment APPROACH (Section 7) — RATHER than IFRS 9'S three-CATEGORY, business-MODEL-driven classification AND its MANDATORY expected CREDIT loss (ECL) model.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Financial assets, financial liabilities, and equity instruments</div><div class="principle">This Section applies TO all FINANCIAL instruments, WORKING alongside SECTION 3051 (INVESTMENTS), Section 3210 (LONG-term debt), and SECTION 3251 (EQUITY) for THEIR own MORE specific PRESENTATION guidance.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>THE CLASSIFICATION MODEL — Two Categories, Not Three</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A significantly simpler structure than IFRS 9</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Category</span><span>Description</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Cost / amortized cost</span><span>MOST receivables, PAYABLES, and SIMPLE debt INSTRUMENTS — the DEFAULT category</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Fair value through net income</span><span>INSTRUMENTS QUOTED in an ACTIVE market (MANDATORY), or OTHER instruments WHERE the FAIR value OPTION is ELECTED</span></div>
</div>
<div class="text-supporting">THIS two-CATEGORY structure is SIGNIFICANTLY simpler THAN IFRS 9'S own THREE-category model (AMORTIZED cost, FAIR value through OCI, and FAIR value through PROFIT or LOSS), WHICH depends ON a detailed BUSINESS-model and CONTRACTUAL cash-FLOW-characteristics assessment — ASPE AVOIDS this COMPLEXITY entirely.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Basic Financial Instruments — Cost or Amortized Cost</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">The default category for most everyday instruments</div><div class="text-supporting">MOST basic FINANCIAL instruments — ACCOUNTS receivable, ACCOUNTS payable, and CONVENTIONAL fixed-OR floating-rate DEBT — are MEASURED at COST or AMORTIZED cost, USING the EFFECTIVE interest METHOD where APPLICABLE (see the COMPANION Section 3210 poster).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Quoted Instruments — Mandatory Fair Value Through Net Income</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">No OCI available, so changes flow directly to the bottom line</div><div class="text-supporting">FINANCIAL instruments QUOTED in an ACTIVE market are MEASURED at FAIR value, WITH changes RECOGNIZED directly WITHIN net INCOME — GIVEN ASPE'S absence OF any OCI MECHANISM (see the COMPANION Section 1520 poster), THERE is no ALTERNATIVE "fair VALUE through OCI" category THE way IFRS 9 offers FOR certain debt AND equity instruments.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>The Elective Fair Value Option</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Available where it reduces measurement inconsistency</div><div class="text-supporting">AN enterprise MAY elect the FAIR value OPTION for OTHER financial INSTRUMENTS (BEYOND those QUOTED in an ACTIVE market) WHERE doing SO eliminates OR significantly REDUCES a measurement OR recognition INCONSISTENCY that WOULD otherwise ARISE from MEASURING related ASSETS and LIABILITIES on DIFFERENT bases.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>THE IMPAIRMENT MODEL — Incurred Loss, Not Expected Credit Loss</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A genuinely significant divergence from IFRS 9 and even from ASBE</div><div class="text-supporting">FOR financial ASSETS measured AT cost OR amortized COST, an IMPAIRMENT loss is RECOGNIZED only WHEN there IS an INDICATION of impairment — TO the EXTENT the CARRYING amount EXCEEDS the HIGHEST of: the PRESENT value OF expected FUTURE cash FLOWS; the AMOUNT that COULD be REALIZED by SELLING the asset; OR the AMOUNT expected TO be REALIZED by EXERCISING rights TO any COLLATERAL. THIS is a GENUINE "INCURRED loss" model — REQUIRING objective EVIDENCE that a LOSS event HAS already OCCURRED, RATHER than IFRS 9'S mandatory EXPECTED credit LOSS (ECL) model (ADOPTED internationally in 2018), WHICH requires FORWARD-looking loss PROVISIONING even BEFORE a SPECIFIC loss EVENT has OCCURRED. NOTABLY, THIS also DIFFERS from ASBE'S own 2018/2021 ADOPTION of an ECL-style MODEL (see the COMPANION Chinese GAAP poster) — ASPE RETAINS the OLDER, incurred-LOSS approach that WAS common INTERNATIONALLY prior TO the post-financial-CRISIS shift TOWARD expected-LOSS provisioning.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Embedded Derivatives — a Genuine Non-Bifurcation Choice</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A practical simplification for certain host contracts</div><div class="text-supporting">ASPE provides an ACCOUNTING policy CHOICE permitting an ENTERPRISE, in CERTAIN circumstances, NOT to SEPARATELY identify and FAIR-value embedded DERIVATIVES within CERTAIN host CONTRACTS — a GENUINELY practical SIMPLIFICATION relative TO the MORE detailed embedded-DERIVATIVE bifurcation RULES that HAVE historically APPLIED, and CONTINUE to apply IN narrower circumstances, INTERNATIONALLY.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Basic Hedge Accounting</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Simplified rules for common hedging relationships</div><div class="text-supporting">ASPE permits BASIC hedge ACCOUNTING for SPECIFIC, common HEDGING relationships (e.g. AN interest RATE swap CONVERTING floating-RATE debt to FIXED-rate, or a FORWARD exchange CONTRACT hedging a FOREIGN currency COMMITMENT), WITH substantially SIMPLIFIED rules RELATIVE to IFRS 9'S own MORE complex hedge-ACCOUNTING model.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Cross-Reference — Retractable or Mandatorily Redeemable Shares</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">The liability classification underlying Section 3251's own relief</div><div class="text-supporting">THIS Section SUPPLIES the UNDERLYING financial-INSTRUMENT classification PRINCIPLES requiring RETRACTABLE or MANDATORILY redeemable SHARES to BE presented AS a financial LIABILITY — WITH Section 3251'S own SPECIFIC relief (CHARGING the RESULTING adjustment TO retained EARNINGS, rather THAN net income) BUILT on TOP of this Section'S general CLASSIFICATION framework (see the COMPANION Section 3251 poster).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Worked Example — the Incurred Loss Impairment Test</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Requiring an actual loss event, not merely a forward-looking risk assessment</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A customer's deteriorating financial position</div><div class="wk-body">A company holds a $400,000 receivable from a customer measured at cost. The customer files for bankruptcy protection during the year — an objective indication of impairment. The present value of expected future cash flows from the receivable is estimated at $220,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">GIVEN the BANKRUPTCY filing IS an OBJECTIVE, ALREADY-occurred LOSS event, the COMPANY recognizes an IMPAIRMENT loss OF $400,000 − $220,000 = <strong>$180,000</strong> — CONSISTENT with the INCURRED-loss model'S REQUIREMENT for OBJECTIVE evidence OF impairment, RATHER than a MERELY forward-LOOKING expected-LOSS assessment.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Worked Example — Classifying a Quoted vs Unquoted Investment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">The simple, binary classification test in action</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Two portfolio investments with different classification outcomes</div><div class="wk-body">A company holds a small stake in a publicly traded company (quoted, active market) and a small stake in an unquoted private company. Neither investment provides significant influence.</div><div class="wk-answer">The PUBLICLY traded STAKE is MANDATORILY measured AT fair VALUE through NET income; the UNQUOTED stake is MEASURED at <strong>COST</strong> (unless the COMPANY elects the FAIR value option) — a SIMPLE, binary CLASSIFICATION test, RATHER than IFRS 9'S more ELABORATE business-MODEL analysis.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What Section 3856 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Significant terms and conditions of financial instruments that may affect amount, timing, and certainty of future cash flows</span><span>Nature and extent of risks arising from financial instruments (credit, liquidity, market risk)</span><span>Basis of measurement applied to each category of financial instrument</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgment matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether objective evidence of impairment genuinely exists</span><span>Whether to elect the fair value option for a given instrument</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Present value of expected future cash flows, for impairment measurement</span><span>Fair value of unquoted instruments, where the fair value option is elected</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS 9</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASPE Section 3856 vs IFRS 9</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASPE Section 3856</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS 9</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Classification categories</span><span>TWO (cost/amortized cost, fair value through net income)</span><span>THREE (amortized cost, FVOCI, FVTPL)</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Impairment model</span><span>Incurred loss</span><span>Expected credit loss (mandatory since 2018)</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">TWO genuinely SIGNIFICANT divergences — THE simpler CLASSIFICATION structure and, ESPECIALLY, the RETAINED incurred-LOSS impairment MODEL, WHICH stands IN meaningful CONTRAST to the POST-financial-crisis global SHIFT toward FORWARD-looking expected-LOSS provisioning.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every financial instrument event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Quoted instrument fair value change</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge is">IS</span></span><span>Recognized directly in net income</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Incurred loss impairment</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge is">IS</span></span><span>Recognized only upon objective evidence of a loss event</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 3210</span><span>Long-term Debt — supplies the amortized cost mechanics this Section's basic instrument category applies</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 3251</span><span>Equity — builds its RoMRS-specific relief on top of this Section's general liability classification principles</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s18"><span class="num">18</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Incurred loss model</div><div class="glossary-def">An impairment approach recognizing a loss only where objective evidence indicates a loss event has already occurred, rather than forward-looking expected loss provisioning.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Basic financial instrument</div><div class="glossary-def">A conventional receivable, payable, or debt instrument measured at cost or amortized cost, the default category under Section 3856.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (ASPE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge is">IS</span> Income Statement </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge sre">SRE</span> Statement of Retained Earnings / Statement of Changes in Equity </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge scf">SCF</span> Cash Flow Statement</div></div></div>


</div>
  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.aspe3856 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
